\ Spitfire: the flight model, in SI units, of a type whose figures
\ aircraft-types.telic gives. For the Mk I the published figures are the
\ loaded weight, 2,624 kg; the wing, 11.23 m across and 22.48 m²; the
\ Merlin III's 1,030 hp (768 kW), held to its full-throttle height of
\ about 5,500 m and falling with the air's density above it. The lift
\ slope, the drag polar and the propeller's efficiency (0.8 from 120 m/s,
\ less when slower) are estimates chosen so the model reaches the published
\ 582 km/h at 5,669 m and climbs near the published 770 m/min. Each step sums
\ the forces — lift at right angles to the airflow, drag against it, the
\ propeller's thrust along the nose, the weight — and moves the aircraft by
\ them; and sums the moments about the centre of gravity and turns the
\ aircraft by them through its moments of inertia. The propeller turns
\ clockwise seen from the cockpit, as the Merlin's did. Its torque, the
\ power over the propeller's turning speed, rolls the aircraft left. Its
\ slipstream, by actuator-disc theory, speeds up and narrows behind it and
\ turns with it, carrying the torque as angular momentum: the swirl rises
\ under the left wing's root and falls on the right's, rolling the aircraft
\ back to the right, never by more than the torque; what turning the wing
\ roots leave in it strikes the fin from the left, yawing the nose left,
\ most at full power and low speed, while the slipstream over the rudder
\ strengthens it. The turning propeller's
\ gyroscopic moment yaws the nose right as it pitches up and pitches it down
\ as it yaws right. A take-off so swings left and needs right rudder, as the
\ Merlin Spitfires' did (their pilot's notes give the Griffon's swing as the
\ opposite). On the ground the aircraft stands on its two main wheels and
\ its tailwheel, each leg a spring and damper, the main tyres gripping
\ sideways and the castoring tailwheel not, the brakes shared out between
\ the main wheels by the rudder bar; at rest the nose stands up at the angle
\ the wheels give, about 11°. Each half of the wing
\ meets the air at its own angle, raised on the half going down as the
\ aircraft rolls and on the half the sideslip meets, through the dihedral,
\ and at its own speed as the aircraft yaws; the halves' lift and drag
\ differ, and roll and yaw the aircraft: so the roll is damped, the wing
\ goes down that stalls first, and past the stall a roll feeds itself, the
\ spin. The ailerons change the halves' lift, and drag the aircraft's nose
\ against the roll; the wing's twist weakens them toward the reversal
\ speed. The tailplane restores the angle of attack and damps the pitch,
\ the fin the sideslip and the yaw; the elevator and rudder move them. The
\ pilot moves each control as far as he asked or as far as his strength
\ against the air allows, so the controls stiffen as the speed grows. The
\ gyroscopic coupling of the three rotations is kept. Past about 15°
\ the wing stalls and lift falls away.
\
\ The fuel is the Spitfire II pilot's notes' 85 gallons, 48 in the upper
\ tank and 37 in the lower, taken as the Mk I's too. The engine burns it in
\ proportion to its power, 86 gallons an hour at full power: an estimate
\ from an assumed 0.6 lb a horsepower-hour at 1,030 hp and 7.2 lb a gallon,
\ not a published figure. With the tanks empty the engine stops. The
\ Hurricane's 97 gallons are its main tanks and a 28-gallon reserve; its
\ fuel cock draws from one or the other, and the engine stops when the
\ tanks it draws from run dry, to run again on the other.
\
\ The flaps are split flaps worked by air, up or down and nothing between
\ (the pilot's notes); they travel in an estimated 2 seconds, adding lift
\ and drag as far as they are down and bringing the nose down, the notes'
\ "strongly nose down" change of trim, which the stick must hold. Each type
\ limits them to its own indicated speed, the Spitfire's notes to 140 mph,
\ the Hurricane's cockpit placard to 120 mph; above an estimated 30 mph
\ more the air blows them back up. The airspeed indicator, like the real one, shows
\ the indicated speed: the true speed scaled by the square root of the
\ air's density over sea level's. The elevator trim's tab, from full
\ nose-down to full nose-up, holds the elevator at up to 0.4 of its travel
\ (an estimate) without the pilot's force, so the stick can rest in the
\ middle at a chosen speed.
\
\ The engine heats its coolant with its power, and the radiator cools it
\ by the air through it, the airspeed and the propeller's wash, against
\ the air's temperature, which falls with height; below 75°C a thermostat
\ (estimated) closes the radiator, shut at 60°C, so the engine stays warm in
\ the cold of 30,000 ft. The coolant settles near
\ 75°C cruising at 1,000 m, reaches 113°C after three minutes' climb at
\ full power, short of the published limit of 120°C, and idling on the
\ ground it passes the limit after about six minutes and boils before ten,
\ as Spitfires overheated taxiing; with the radiator holed it boils within
\ two minutes. The oil's temperature follows the
\ coolant's; its pressure rises with the revolutions and falls as the oil
\ warms. A hit can hole the glycol radiator or the oil system: as the
\ coolant leaks the radiator cools less, and as the oil leaks the pressure
\ falls. Running above 120°C or short of oil pressure wears the engine,
\ and worn out it seizes. Every figure but the 120°C limit is an estimate.
\
\ The throttle sets the boost, from -4 lb closed to the published +6 lb
\ open; the boost cut-out, on 100-octane fuel from 1940, raises it to the
\ published +12 lb for a published five minutes, past which the engine
\ wears. The supercharger holds the boost only up to its ceiling at the
\ height, the rated boost's at the full-throttle height and less above;
\ above it the closed throttle's pressure falls in the same proportion.
\ The pressure at the closed throttle is taken as the engine's friction, so
\ the power is the throttle's opening times the open manifold's pressure
\ over the rated boost's times the revolutions over the rated 3,000: none
\ closed, 1,030 hp open at +6 lb and 1,328 hp at +12 lb, against the
\ published 1,310. The 1939 Spitfire's two-pitch
\ propeller turns at revolutions that follow the boost and the airspeed,
\ idling near 600 rpm with the throttle closed on the ground (an estimate),
\ fine for taking off and climbing, coarse for speed, and over-revs past
\ 3,000 at speed in fine pitch; the constant-speed propeller fitted from
\ June 1940 holds the revolutions the pilot sets, 1,800 to 3,000 rpm.
\
\ The engine is stopped as the pilot's notes stop it, with the slow-running
\ cut-out, which shuts off its fuel at the carburettor: held for an
\ estimated 2 seconds, it starves the engine and it stops. It runs only with
\ its magnetos on, and switching them off stops it at once. On the ground
\ it is started with the electric starter: held, it turns
\ the engine over, and with the magnetos on and fuel in the tanks the
\ engine fires after an estimated 2.5 seconds' turning and settles to idle.
\ In the air the starter is not used; switching the magnetos back on while
\ the propeller still windmills fast enough restarts the engine.
\
\ The undercarriage travels up or down over an estimated 6 seconds, its drag
\ following its position; touching down before it locks down is a crash.
\
\ On the ground the aircraft rolls on its wheels, tail down until the
\ elevator in the slipstream and the speed lift the tail, until the wing
\ carries its weight. Touching down with the wheels not locked down, too
\ fast downward, or banked so a wing tip meets the ground, or touching water
\ or a hill, is a crash; so is the propeller striking the ground, as it
\ does if the tail rises too far.

9.81 constant gravity
0.012 constant gear-drag
\ the propeller, turning clockwise seen from the cockpit as the Merlin's did: its disc, 1.64 m in radius; the share of the fin and rudder in its slipstream; the fin's centre's height above the hub and the wing roots' distance out at the fuselage's side, metres, where the slipstream's swirl meets them; its moment of inertia, kg m²; the reduction gear (estimates but the radius and the gear)
1.64 sq PI * constant propeller-disc
0.5 constant slipstream-share
0.6 constant fin-swirl-height
0.5 constant wing-root-out
60 constant propeller-inertia
0.477 constant propeller-gear

\ the propeller's slipstream by actuator-disc theory, at the speed through the air, the thrust and the propeller's torque: the wake's speed far behind, the root of the speed squared and twice the thrust over the air's density and the disc; its radius, the disc's narrowed to carry the same flow at the wake's speed from the disc's, halfway between; its swirl, turning as a solid body with the propeller, carrying the torque as the flow's angular momentum, at a rate twice the torque over the flow and the wake's radius squared; [ wake-speed wake-radius swirl-rate ]
: slipstream ( speed thrust torque density -- wake )
	| speed thrust torque density |
	thrust 2 * density propeller-disc * / speed sq + sqrt to wake-speed
	speed wake-speed + 2 / to disc-speed
	density propeller-disc * disc-speed * 0.1 max2 to flow
	propeller-disc PI / sqrt disc-speed wake-speed / sqrt * to wake-radius
	torque 2 * flow wake-radius sq * / to swirl-rate
	[ wake-speed wake-radius swirl-rate ] ;

\ the rolling moment the swirl gives the wing roots inside the wake, right positive: each strip of the roots from the fuselage's side to the wake's edge meets the swirl at its radius over the wake's speed, no more than the stall angle, its lift changed by that over the wake's dynamic pressure, raised on the left where the swirl rises and lowered on the right; no more than the torque the swirl carries, as the wing cannot take out more turning than the wake has
: swirl-roll ( wake density chord lift-slope stall-angle torque -- moment )
	| wake density chord lift-slope stall-angle torque |
	wake spread to swirl-rate to wake-radius to wake-speed
	0.5 density * wake-speed sq * to wake-pressure
	wake-radius wing-root-out - 0 max2 6 / to strip
	0 to moment
	0 6 1 do k
		wing-root-out k 0.5 + strip * + to r
		swirl-rate r * wake-speed 1 max2 / stall-angle min2 to angle
		moment 2 wake-pressure * chord * lift-slope * angle * r * strip * + to moment
	loop
	moment torque min2 ;
\ the seconds the undercarriage takes to travel fully up or down (an estimate)
6 constant undercarriage-travel-time
0.03 constant flap-drag
0.35 constant flap-lift
\ the engine's heat and cooling: the coolant's heating at full power over the cooling's coefficient, °C × m/s; the propeller's wash as airspeed at full revolutions, m/s; the coolant's capacity over the cooling's coefficient, seconds × m/s; the published coolant limit and the estimated temperature at which the glycol boils away, °C
12400 constant engine-heat
30 constant propeller-wash
11160 constant coolant-capacity
120 constant coolant-limit
135 constant coolant-boiling
\ the oil: its temperature following the coolant's over 120 s; its pressure, lb a square inch, at full revolutions when cool, and the pressure below which the bearings wear
120 constant oil-lag
80 constant oil-full-pressure
20 constant oil-low-pressure
\ the elevator trim: the share of the elevator's travel its tab holds at either end of its travel
0.4 constant trim-range
\ the pilot's hand and feet: the share of a control's travel he moves it a second (an estimate)
5 constant control-speed
\ the flaps: their travel, seconds; how far above a type's limit the air blows them back up, mph (an estimate)
2 constant flap-travel-time
30 constant flap-blow-margin

\ the indicated speed, m/s, above which a type's flaps may not be lowered, and the one above which the air blows them back up
: flap-limit-speed ( plane -- speed ) :type @ :flap-limit @ 2.23694 / ;
: flap-blow-speed ( plane -- speed ) :type @ :flap-limit @ flap-blow-margin + 2.23694 / ;
1.6 constant wheel-height
\ the boost, lb a square inch above the standard atmosphere's 14.7: with the throttle closed (an estimate), open, and open with the boost cut-out pushed forward on 100-octane fuel (the published +6 and +12); the emergency boost's published limit, seconds
-4 constant idle-boost
6 constant rated-boost
12 constant emergency-boost
300 constant emergency-limit
14.7 constant atmosphere
\ the propeller's revolutions a minute: the rated 3,000; the two-pitch propeller's at full throttle standing still, fine and coarse, and the airspeed, m/s, that adds as much again (estimates); the constant-speed propeller's lowest setting
3000 constant rated-revolutions
2650 constant fine-revolutions
2100 constant coarse-revolutions
568 constant windmill-speed
1800 constant lowest-revolutions
\ the two-pitch propeller's least revolutions with the throttle closed, a share of the rated: 600 rpm, the constant-speed propeller's idle (an estimate)
0.2 constant two-pitch-idle
\ the electric starter: the seconds it turns the engine over before it fires, the share of the rated revolutions it turns it at, and the windmilling revolutions above which switching the magnetos back on in the air restarts it (estimates)
2.5 constant starter-catch-time
\ the seconds the slow-running cut-out must be held before the starved engine stops (an estimate)
2 constant cut-out-time
0.04 constant cranking-revolutions
0.25 constant restart-revolutions
\ a gallon of petrol's mass, kg: 7.2 lb
3.27 constant fuel-mass
86 constant full-power-flow

variable plane
variable controls
{ :pitch 0 :roll 0 :yaw 0 :brakes false } to controls

\ the aircraft's mass, kg: empty, with the pilot, guns or cameras, and the fuel left
: aircraft-mass ( plane -- kg )
	| plane |
	plane@empty-mass plane@fuel fuel-mass * + plane :ballast null @or null? if 0 else plane@ballast first then + ;

\ the aircraft's centre of gravity in its own terms: the type's, moved toward any ballast it carries by the ballast's share of the mass
: centre-of-mass ( plane -- point )
	| plane |
	plane@type to type
	plane :ballast null @or to ballast
	ballast null? if type@centre exit then
	ballast spread to place to ballast-mass
	type@centre type@mass v* place ballast-mass v* v+ type@mass ballast-mass + 1 swap / v* ;

\ the air's density at a height, kg/m³
: air-density ( height -- density ) -8500 / exp 1.225 * ;

\ the most absolute pressure the supercharger can hold in the manifold at a height, lb a square inch: the rated boost at the full-throttle height, more below it and less above, with the air's density
: manifold-ceiling ( height rated-height -- pressure )
	| height rated-height |
	atmosphere rated-boost + height air-density rated-height air-density / * ;

\ the wind 10 m above the ground, the air's motion over it, metres a second east, up and north; the mission sets it
variable surface-wind
[ 0 0 0 ] to surface-wind

\ the 10 m wind blowing from a bearing, degrees, at a speed, metres a second
: use-wind ( from speed -- )
	| from speed ^surface-wind |
	from PI 180 / * to angle
	[ angle sin speed * negate 0 angle cos speed * negate ] to surface-wind ;

\ the ground's roughness lengths, metres: open sea, and open grassland and airfields (Wieringa's classes, as the log wind profile's literature gives them); the boundary layer's top, above which the wind no longer grows, metres (an estimate)
0.0002 constant sea-roughness
0.03 constant land-roughness
1000 constant boundary-layer-top

\ the share of the 10 m wind blowing at a height above the ground over a roughness: the logarithmic profile, ln(z/z0) over ln(10/z0), the height no less than twice the roughness and no more than the boundary layer's top
: wind-share ( height roughness -- share )
	| height roughness |
	height roughness 2 * max2 boundary-layer-top min2 roughness / ln 10 roughness / ln / ;

\ the aircraft's motion through the air: its motion over the ground less the wind at its height and the gust it is in
: air-velocity ( plane -- velocity )
	| plane |
	plane@velocity plane@local-wind v- plane@gust v- ;

\ the carrier's flight deck the mission may have, or null: a frame of the ship's centre at the waterline, its heading in degrees and speed in metres a second, the deck's height above the water, its ends' distances along the ship from the centre, fore positive, and its half width
variable deck
null to deck

\ the carrier's velocity over the ground
: deck-velocity ( -- velocity ) deck@heading heading-attitude :forward @ deck@speed v* ;

\ the carrier steaming on for a time
: move-deck ( dt -- )
	| dt |
	deck null? if exit then
	deck deck@centre deck-velocity dt v* v+ :centre ! drop ;

\ whether a point lies over the carrier's flight deck
: on-deck? ( point -- flag )
	| point |
	deck null? if false exit then
	point deck@centre v- to offset
	deck@heading heading-attitude to attitude
	offset attitude@forward v-dot to along
	offset attitude@right v-dot abs to across
	along deck@aft >= along deck@fore <= and across deck@half-width <= and ;

\ the velocity of the surface the aircraft rests on: the carrier's on its deck, else none
: surface-velocity ( plane -- velocity )
	| plane |
	plane@on-deck if deck-velocity else [ 0 0 0 ] then ;

\ the clouds the air is in, which the game sets each frame: the cumulus's base, metres, the share of the sky they cover, and whether the aircraft is inside one
variable cloud-air
{ :base 1500 :cover 0 :inside false } to cloud-air

\ the turbulence above 2,000 ft: the gusts' spread, feet a second, at heights in feet, MIL-F-8785C's figure 7 curves as JSBSim's FGWinds.cpp tabulates them: in clear air the spread exceeded a fifth of the time, probability of exceedance 2 × 10^-1; inside a cumulus moderate turbulence, 10^-3
[ 500 1750 3750 7500 15000 25000 35000 45000 ] constant turbulence-heights
[ 3.2 2.2 1.5 0 0 0 0 0 ] constant clear-air-turbulence
[ 8.6 9.6 10.6 10.1 8.0 6.6 5.0 4.2 ] constant moderate-turbulence

\ a table's value at a height, between the two heights either side, held at the ends
: table-at ( heights values height -- value )
	| heights values height |
	height heights first <= if values first exit then
	height heights last >= if values last exit then
	0 heights size 1- 1 do k
		height heights k 1+ @i < if
			height heights k @i - heights k 1+ @i heights k @i - / to share
			values k @i values k 1+ @i values k @i - share * + to value
			leave
		then
	loop
	value ;

\ the Dryden turbulence model's figures at a height above the ground, feet, with the wind 20 ft up, feet a second, as MIL-F-8785C gives them, [ along-spread vertical-spread along-scale vertical-scale ] in feet a second and feet: below 1,000 ft the vertical scale the height, the along and across scales the height over (0.177 + 0.000823 h)^1.2, the vertical spread a tenth of the 20 ft wind and the others it over (0.177 + 0.000823 h)^0.4; above 2,000 ft the scales 1,750 ft and the spreads the table's; between, each figure in proportion
: dryden-figures ( height wind-20 table -- figures )
	| height wind-20 table |
	height 10 max2 to low-height
	low-height 1000 min2 to h
	0.177 0.000823 h * + to factor
	[ wind-20 0.1 * factor 0.4 ^ / wind-20 0.1 * h factor 1.2 ^ / h ] to low
	turbulence-heights table low-height 2000 max2 table-at to high-spread
	[ high-spread high-spread 1750 1750 ] to high
	low-height 1000 - 1000 / 0 1 clamp to share
	high low v- share v* low v+ ;

\ the convective turbulence below the cumulus's base: the vertical gusts' spread, metres a second, at a height under a base, by Lenschow's profile, 1.8 (z/zi)^(2/3) (1 - 0.8 z/zi)^2 times the convective velocity squared, that velocity 2 m/s under a sky full of cumulus and in proportion to the cover (an estimate); none above the base
: convective-spread ( height base cover -- spread )
	| height base cover |
	height base >= cover 0 <= or if 0 exit then
	height 0 max2 base / to depth-share
	2 cover * to convective-velocity
	1.8 depth-share 2 3 / ^ * 1 0.8 depth-share * - sq * sqrt convective-velocity * ;

\ one step of a unit gust's filters, [ along across across-lag vertical vertical-lag ]: each first-order filter of a time constant, the along one the gust itself, the across and vertical ones followed by a second of the same constant, as the Dryden spectra's shapes have it, every filter's output spread kept at one
: step-gust-filters ( filters along-time vertical-time dt -- filters' )
	| filters along-time vertical-time dt |
	filters spread to vertical-lag to vertical to across-lag to across to along
	dt along-time / negate exp to along-keep
	dt vertical-time / negate exp to vertical-keep
	along along-keep * 1 along-keep sq - sqrt random-normal * + to along
	across along-keep * 1 along-keep sq - sqrt random-normal * + to across
	across-lag across across-lag - 1 along-keep - * + to across-lag
	vertical vertical-keep * 1 vertical-keep sq - sqrt random-normal * + to vertical
	vertical-lag vertical vertical-lag - 1 vertical-keep - * + to vertical-lag
	[ along across across-lag vertical vertical-lag ] ;

\ the air round the aircraft for a step: the wind at its height above the ground, over the sea's roughness or the land's; the gust it is in, the Dryden model's along the air's path, across it and up, its spreads and scales for the height, the wind 20 ft up and the clouds, their filters' time constants the scales over the airspeed, the vertical spread joined by the convective spread below the cumulus
: move-air ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@position spread to z to y to x
	y x z ground-height - to height
	x z water-at? if sea-roughness else land-roughness then to roughness
	surface-wind height roughness wind-share v* plane!local-wind
	surface-wind v-length 6.1 roughness wind-share * 3.28084 * to wind-20
	cloud-air@inside if moderate-turbulence else clear-air-turbulence then to table
	height 3.28084 * wind-20 table dryden-figures spread to vertical-scale to along-scale to vertical-spread to along-spread
	along-spread 0.3048 * to along-spread
	vertical-spread 0.3048 * sq height cloud-air@base cloud-air@cover convective-spread sq + sqrt to vertical-spread
	plane@velocity plane@local-wind v- to relative
	relative v-length 5 max2 to airspeed
	[ relative first 0 relative 2 @i ] to level
	level v-length 0.1 < if [ 0 0 1 ] else level v-unit then to along
	[ along 2 @i 0 along first negate ] to across
	plane@gust-filters along-scale 0.3048 * airspeed / vertical-scale 0.3048 * airspeed / dt step-gust-filters to filters
	plane filters :gust-filters ! drop
	filters spread to vertical-lag to vertical to across-lag to across-gust to along-gust
	3 sqrt to root-three
	root-three across-gust * 1 root-three - across-lag * + 2 sqrt / along-spread * to across-speed
	root-three vertical * 1 root-three - vertical-lag * + 2 sqrt / vertical-spread * to vertical-speed
	along along-gust along-spread * v* across across-speed v* v+ [ 0 vertical-speed 0 ] v+ plane!gust ;

\ the undercarriage's legs: each main leg's compression under its share of the weight at rest and the tailwheel leg's, metres, and the share of critical damping in each; the tyres' rolling friction, the brakes' greatest and the main tyres' sideways grip, as shares of a wheel's load (estimates; the rolling and braking shares the decelerations the model had before the wheels were modelled)
0.1 constant main-leg-compression
0.05 constant tail-leg-compression
0.5 constant leg-damping
0.04 constant rolling-friction
0.45 constant braking-friction
0.6 constant tyre-grip

\ the tyre's slip angle, radians, at which its sideways grip reaches its whole, growing in proportion to it below (an estimate)
0.12 constant full-grip-slip

\ the height of the surface under a point: the carrier's deck over it, else the ground
: surface-height ( point -- height )
	| point |
	point on-deck? if deck@height exit then
	point first point 2 @i ground-height ;

\ a point in the aircraft's own terms, [ right up forward ] from its centre of gravity, turned into the ground's terms from the centre of gravity
: body-arm ( plane point -- arm )
	| plane point |
	plane@attitude to attitude
	point plane centre-of-mass v- spread to along to up to out
	attitude@right out v* attitude@up up v* v+ attitude@forward along v* v+ ;

\ the undercarriage's wheels, each [ centre radius share compression grips brake ]: the left and right main wheels, each carrying half of what the tailwheel does not, gripping sideways, each braked by the brakes' lever as the rudder bar shares the braking out, the right one more as the bar goes right, as the Spitfire's and Hurricane's differential brakes worked; the castoring tailwheel, free sideways and unbraked
: wheels ( plane -- wheels )
	| plane |
	plane@type to type
	type@tail-share to tail-share
	type@main-wheel spread to along to up to out
	controls@yaw to pedal
	1 tail-share - 2 / to main-share
	[ [ [ out negate up along ] type@wheel-radius main-share main-leg-compression true 1 pedal - 0 1 clamp ]
	  [ [ out up along ] type@wheel-radius main-share main-leg-compression true 1 pedal + 0 1 clamp ]
	  [ type@tail-wheel type@tail-wheel-radius tail-share tail-leg-compression false 0 ] ] ;

\ the wheels' forces on the aircraft and their moments about its centre of gravity, [ roll pitch yaw ] as the air's, and how many wheels touch: each wheel pressed into the surface beneath it a spring stiff enough to compress its leg by the leg's figure under its share of the weight, and a damper of the leg's share of the critical damping; along the wheel's rolling, against its motion over the surface, the rolling friction and the braking; across, the tyre's grip, a main wheel's only, growing with the slip angle, the wheel's sideways speed over its rolling speed; each force's moment its arm from the centre of gravity crossed with it
: wheel-forces ( plane -- force moments touching )
	| plane |
	plane@attitude to attitude
	plane@rates spread to yaw-rate to pitch-rate to roll-rate
	attitude@right pitch-rate negate v* attitude@forward roll-rate negate v* v+ attitude@up yaw-rate v* v+ to spin
	plane aircraft-mass gravity * to weight
	plane surface-velocity to carried
	[ attitude@forward first 0 attitude@forward 2 @i ] to ahead
	ahead v-length 0.01 < if [ 0 0 1 ] else ahead v-unit then to ahead
	[ ahead 2 @i 0 ahead first negate ] to beside
	[ 0 0 0 ] to force
	[ 0 0 0 ] to torque
	0 to touching
	plane wheels to all-wheels
	0 all-wheels size 1 do k
		all-wheels k @i spread to brake to grips to compression to share to radius to place
		plane place body-arm to arm
		plane@position arm v+ [ 0 radius 0 ] v- to contact
		contact surface-height contact second - to depth
		depth 0 > if
			plane@velocity spin arm v-cross v+ carried v- to point-velocity
			weight share * compression / to stiffness
			stiffness weight share * gravity / * sqrt 2 * leg-damping * to damper
			stiffness depth * damper point-velocity second * - 0 max2 to normal
			rolling-friction braking-friction controls@brakes if brake else 0 then * + to friction
			friction normal * point-velocity ahead v-dot 0.5 / -1 1 clamp * negate to drag
			point-velocity beside v-dot point-velocity ahead v-dot abs 1 max2 / to slip-angle
			grips if tyre-grip normal * slip-angle full-grip-slip / -1 1 clamp * negate else 0 then to side-force
			[ 0 normal 0 ] ahead drag v* v+ beside side-force v* v+ to push
			force push v+ to force
			torque arm push v-cross v+ to torque
			touching 1+ to touching
		then
	loop
	force
	[ torque attitude@forward v-dot negate torque attitude@right v-dot negate torque attitude@up v-dot ]
	touching ;

\ the aircraft at rest on its wheels: its nose raised by the angle at which the main wheels and the tailwheel touch together, the main legs compressed by their figure under its weight
: rest-on-ground ( plane -- )
	| plane |
	plane@type to type
	type@main-wheel to main
	type@tail-wheel to tail
	tail second type@tail-wheel-radius - main second type@wheel-radius - - main 2 @i tail 2 @i - atan2 to ground-angle
	plane@attitude@forward heading-of heading-attitude :right ground-angle negate turn-attitude plane!attitude
	plane main body-arm to arm
	plane@position surface-height main-leg-compression - type@wheel-radius + arm second - to height
	plane [ plane@position first height plane@position 2 @i ] :position ! drop
	plane [ 0 0 0 ] :rates ! drop ;

\ a new aircraft: where it is, which way it faces, how fast it goes along its nose
: new-plane ( position heading speed on-ground -- plane )
	| position heading speed on-ground |
	heading heading-attitude to attitude
	spitfire-mk-i to type
	{ :type type
	  :position position
	  :velocity attitude@forward speed v*
	  :attitude attitude
	  :rates [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :stick [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :deflections [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :throttle on-ground if 0 else 0.8 then
	  :revolutions on-ground if 0 else 0.8 then
	  :magnetos on-ground not
	  :running on-ground not
	  :cranking false
	  :starter-time 0
	  :cut-out-held 0
	  :cut-out-stopped false
	  :propeller :constant-speed
	  :fine true
	  :revolution-setting rated-revolutions
	  :boost-cut-out false
	  :emergency-allowed true
	  :emergency-time 0
	  :boost 0
	  :power 0
	  :flaps false
	  :flap-setting 0
	  :flaps-blown false
	  :trim 0
	  :rudder-trim 0
	  :coolant-temperature on-ground if 60 else 90 then
	  :oil-temperature on-ground if 50 else 70 then
	  :oil-pressure 60
	  :coolant-left 1
	  :oil-left 1
	  :coolant-leak 0
	  :oil-leak 0
	  :engine-wear 0
	  :engine-failure ""
	  :gear on-ground
	  :gear-position on-ground if 1 else 0 then
	  :on-ground on-ground
	  :crashed false
	  :crash-reason ""
	  :angle-of-attack 0
	  :g-load 1
	  :g-felt 1
	  :gust [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :local-wind [ 0 0 0 ]
	  :gust-filters [ 0 0 0 0 0 ]
	  :on-deck false
	  :propeller-angle 0
	  :turn-rate 0
	  :fuel type@fuel-capacity
	  :fuel-capacity type@fuel-capacity
	  :reserve-fuel type@reserve-capacity
	  :fuel-cock :main
	  :fuel-starved false
	  :empty-mass type@mass type@fuel-capacity fuel-mass * -
	  :rated-power type@engine-power
	  :full-throttle-height type@full-throttle-height
	  :armed true
	  :ammunition type@fire-seconds
	  :damage 0
	  :engine-dead false
	} to aircraft
	on-ground if aircraft rest-on-ground then
	aircraft ;

\ the lift coefficient at an angle of attack, the flaps adding to it as far as they are down (0 up to 1 down); beyond the stall it falls away at the type's post-stall slope
: lift-coefficient ( angle flap-setting type -- coefficient )
	| angle flap-setting type |
	type@stall-angle to stall
	flap-lift flap-setting * to flap-boost
	angle abs stall <= if
		type@lift-slope angle * type@zero-lift-coefficient + flap-boost + exit
	then
	angle 0 > if 1 else -1 then to sign
	type@lift-slope stall * type@zero-lift-coefficient + flap-boost + to peak
	peak angle abs stall - type@post-stall-slope * - 0.5 max2 sign * ;

\ the pilot's hands and feet: the stick and rudder bar moved toward where he asks, at most control-speed of their travel a second; each surface deflected as far as he moves it or as far as his greatest force against the air holds it, [ ailerons elevator rudder ] as shares of their travel
: move-controls ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@type to type
	plane@position second air-density 0.5 * plane air-velocity v-length sq * 1 max2 to pressure
	[ controls@roll controls@pitch controls@yaw ] plane@stick v- to error
	control-speed dt * to reach
	plane@stick [ error first reach negate reach clamp error second reach negate reach clamp error 2 @i reach negate reach clamp ] v+ to stick
	stick plane!stick
	aileron-force type@aileron-stiffness pressure * / 1 min2 to aileron-reach
	elevator-force type@elevator-stiffness pressure * / 1 min2 to elevator-reach
	rudder-force type@rudder-stiffness pressure * / 1 min2 to rudder-reach
	[ stick first aileron-reach negate aileron-reach clamp
	  stick second elevator-reach negate elevator-reach clamp
	  stick 2 @i rudder-reach negate rudder-reach clamp ] plane!deflections ;

\ the forces on the aircraft, ground frame, newtons, and the moments about its centre of gravity, [ roll pitch yaw ] newton-metres rolling right, pitching up and yawing right. Each half of the wing, left then right, meets the air at the angle of attack raised by the roll on the half going down and by the sideslip through the dihedral on the half it meets, at the speed changed by the yaw, its lift less the aileron's on the right as the stick goes right, the ailerons weakened toward the reversal's dynamic pressure; the halves' lift and drag act at the half ellipse's centroid. The pitching moment: the type's offset, the wing's lift about the centre of gravity, the tail's restoring slope, the pitch damping, the elevator with the trim tab's share, the flaps, and past the stall angle the type's pitch-down. The yawing moment: the halves' drag, the fin's stability against the sideslip and damping of the yaw, the rudder with the rudder bias's share, the slipstream's swirl on the fin. The propeller's torque and the swirl on the wing roots roll it; its gyroscopic moment couples pitch and yaw. Its thrust's centre moves off the hub where the air meets the disc at an angle, the blade turning into the air's cross-flow seeing it faster: by the cross-flow's speed over the propeller's turning speed, clockwise from the cross-flow's direction seen from behind, so the nose-up airflow moves it right and yaws the nose left and a sideslip from the left moves it down and pitches the nose up (only the faster air reckoned; the blades' changed angle of attack, which their pitch and section would give, would add to it)
: aerodynamics ( plane -- force moments )
	| plane |
	plane@type to type
	plane air-velocity to velocity
	velocity v-length to speed
	plane@attitude to attitude
	plane@position second air-density to density
	0.5 density * speed sq * to dynamic-pressure
	attitude velocity ground>attitude spread to ahead to rising to across
	rising negate ahead 0.1 max2 atan2 to angle
	across ahead 0.1 max2 atan2 to slip
	angle plane!angle-of-attack
	plane@power plane@rated-power * to power
	speed 120 / 1 min2 0.01 max2 0.4 ^ type@propeller-efficiency * to efficiency
	power efficiency * speed 1 max2 / type@static-thrust plane@revolutions * min2 to thrust
	attitude@forward thrust v* [ 0 plane aircraft-mass gravity * negate 0 ] v+ to pull-and-weight
	speed 0.5 <= if
		pull-and-weight [ 0 0 0 ] exit
	then
	plane@rates spread to yaw-rate to pitch-rate to roll-rate
	plane@deflections spread to rudder to elevator to aileron
	plane@flap-setting to flaps
	type@wing-area to area
	type@span to span
	type@chord to chord
	type@panel-arm to arm
	1 dynamic-pressure type@reversal-pressure / - to twist
	type@parasite-drag gear-drag plane@gear-position * + flap-drag flaps * + to base-drag
	[ 0 0 ] to lifts
	[ 0 0 ] to drags
	[ 0 0 ] to coefficients
	0 2 1 do k
		k 2 * 1 - to side
		angle side roll-rate * arm * speed / + side type@dihedral * slip * + to half-angle
		speed side yaw-rate * arm * - speed / sq to speed-share
		half-angle flaps type lift-coefficient side aileron * type@aileron-lift * twist * - to half-lift
		dynamic-pressure speed-share * area * 0.5 * to half-force
		lifts half-force half-lift * k !i drop
		drags half-force base-drag type@induced-drag half-lift sq * + * k !i drop
		coefficients half-lift k !i drop
	loop
	velocity v-unit to airflow
	attitude@up airflow attitude@up airflow v-dot v* v- v-unit to lift-direction
	lift-direction lifts first lifts second + v* to lift
	airflow drags first drags second + negate v* to drag
	attitude@right across ahead 0.1 max2 / dynamic-pressure * area * -0.6 * v* to sideways
	lifts first lifts second - arm * to roll-moment
	coefficients first coefficients second + 2 / to wing-lift
	type@pitch-offset
	type@wing-arm wing-lift * +
	type@tail-slope angle * -
	type@pitch-damping pitch-rate chord * 2 speed * / * +
	type@elevator-power elevator plane@trim trim-range * + * +
	type@flap-moment flaps * +
	type@stall-pitch-down angle type@stall-angle - 0 max2 * + dynamic-pressure area * chord * * to pitch-moment
	slipstream-share thrust * propeller-disc / to slipstream-pressure
	plane@revolutions rated-revolutions * propeller-gear * TAU * 60 / 10 max2 to propeller-speed
	power propeller-speed / to torque
	speed thrust torque density slipstream to wake
	wake spread to swirl-rate drop to wake-speed
	wake density chord type@lift-slope type@stall-angle torque swirl-roll to root-roll
	1 root-roll torque 1 max2 / - to swirl-left
	swirl-rate swirl-left * fin-swirl-height * wake-speed 1 max2 / type@stall-angle min2 to fin-swirl
	propeller-inertia propeller-speed * to spin
	type@yaw-stability slip *
	type@yaw-damping yaw-rate span * 2 speed * / * + dynamic-pressure area * span * *
	type@rudder-power rudder plane@rudder-trim trim-range * + * dynamic-pressure slipstream-pressure + area * span * * +
	type@yaw-stability fin-swirl * dynamic-pressure slipstream-pressure + * area * span * -
	spin pitch-rate * +
	drags second drags first - arm * + to yaw-moment
	pitch-moment spin yaw-rate * - to pitch-moment
	roll-moment torque - root-roll + to roll-moment
	rising negate propeller-speed / to thrust-right
	across propeller-speed / to thrust-up
	yaw-moment thrust thrust-right * - to yaw-moment
	pitch-moment thrust thrust-up * - to pitch-moment
	lift drag v+ sideways v+ pull-and-weight v+
	[ roll-moment pitch-moment yaw-moment ] ;

\ the engine's starting and stopping: with the slow-running cut-out held (controls :cut-out) its fuel is shut off at the carburettor and after the cut-out's time it stops, marked as stopped by the cut-out; with the magnetos off it stops at once; on the ground, with the starter held (controls :starter) and the engine not running, the starter turns it over, and after the starter's catch time with the magnetos on and fuel in the tanks the cock draws from it fires and runs; in the air, with the magnetos switched back on while the propeller still windmills fast enough, it runs again
: run-starter ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	controls :cut-out false @or plane@running and if plane@cut-out-held dt + else 0 then plane!cut-out-held
	plane@cut-out-held cut-out-time >= if
		false plane!running
		true plane!cut-out-stopped
		0 plane!cut-out-held
	then
	plane@magnetos not if false plane!running then
	plane@running if false plane!cut-out-stopped then
	plane@running not plane@on-ground and controls :starter false @or and to cranking
	cranking plane!cranking
	cranking if plane@starter-time dt + else 0 then plane!starter-time
	plane@engine-dead plane@fuel-starved or if exit then
	plane@running plane@magnetos not or if exit then
	cranking plane@starter-time starter-catch-time >= and if true plane!running exit then
	plane@on-ground not plane@revolutions restart-revolutions >= and if true plane!running then ;

\ the throttle, the propeller and the power: the throttle sets the manifold's pressure between the closed throttle's and the open's, and the boost cut-out raises the open boost to the emergency's; the supercharger holds the open pressure only up to its ceiling at the height, and the closed pressure falls with it above the full-throttle height. The constant-speed propeller's governor holds the revolutions at their setting, idling below a tenth of the throttle; the two-pitch propeller's revolutions follow the throttle's opening between the closed and the rated boost, and the airspeed, in fine or coarse pitch, no fewer than its idle's. The power, as a share of the rated, is the throttle's opening times the open manifold's pressure over the rated boost's times the revolutions over the rated. Running over the rated revolutions, or on emergency boost past its five minutes, wears the engine. An engine starved of fuel makes no power, as a dead one does, until the cock turns to a tank that has some; nor one not running, which the starter, while cranking, turns slowly
: run-propeller ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane air-velocity v-length to speed
	plane@position second to height
	plane@boost-cut-out if emergency-boost else rated-boost then to open-boost
	plane dt run-starter
	plane@engine-dead plane@fuel-starved or plane@running not or to stopped
	stopped if
		atmosphere height air-density 1.225 / * to manifold
		plane@cranking if cranking-revolutions else 0 then to target
	else
		height plane@full-throttle-height manifold-ceiling to ceiling
		atmosphere open-boost + ceiling min2 to open-manifold
		atmosphere idle-boost + ceiling atmosphere rated-boost + / 1 min2 * to closed-manifold
		open-manifold closed-manifold - plane@throttle * closed-manifold + to manifold
		plane@propeller :constant-speed = if
			plane@throttle 0.1 < if 0.2 else plane@revolution-setting rated-revolutions / then to target
		else
			manifold atmosphere idle-boost + - rated-boost idle-boost - / 0 1 clamp to opening
			plane@fine if fine-revolutions else coarse-revolutions then speed windmill-speed / 1 + *
			opening 0.75 * 0.25 + * rated-revolutions / two-pitch-idle max2 to target
		then
	then
	manifold atmosphere - plane!boost
	plane@revolutions target over - dt 0.8 * * + plane!revolutions
	stopped if 0 else plane@throttle open-manifold * atmosphere rated-boost + / plane@revolutions * then plane!power
	stopped if exit then
	plane@revolutions 1 > if
		plane@engine-wear plane@revolutions 1 - 0.2 / dt * 60 / + plane!engine-wear
	then
	plane@boost-cut-out plane@throttle 0.9 > and if
		plane@emergency-time dt + plane!emergency-time
		plane@emergency-time emergency-limit > if plane@engine-wear dt 120 / + plane!engine-wear then
	then ;

\ the aircraft made a type, its figures aircraft-types.telic's: its engine, its tanks full, its mass, its guns' seconds of fire
: use-type ( plane type -- )
	| plane type |
	type plane!type
	type@engine-power plane!rated-power
	type@full-throttle-height plane!full-throttle-height
	type@fuel-capacity plane!fuel-capacity
	type@fuel-capacity plane!fuel
	type@reserve-capacity plane!reserve-fuel
	type@mass type@fuel-capacity fuel-mass * - plane!empty-mass
	type@fire-seconds plane!ammunition
	plane@on-ground if plane rest-on-ground then ;

\ the aircraft made the photographic reconnaissance PR IV, unarmed
: make-reconnaissance ( plane -- )
	| plane |
	plane spitfire-pr-iv use-type
	false plane!armed
	0 plane!ammunition ;

\ the speed the airspeed indicator shows: the true speed scaled by the square root of the air's density over sea level's, m/s
: indicated-speed ( plane -- speed )
	| plane |
	plane air-velocity v-length plane@position second air-density 1.225 / sqrt * ;

\ the g the pilot's eyes feel: the aircraft's g reached over seconds, as the blood's pressure falls, rising with a 3-second time constant and recovering with a 2-second one (estimates); grey-out from 4 g and black-out at 5 g, the RAF's figures for the average pilot after five seconds (A.P. 129: black-out between 4.1 and 6 g, grey-out about 1 g less)
4 constant grey-out-g
5 constant black-out-g
: feel-g ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane :g-felt 1 @or to felt
	plane@g-load felt > if 3 else 2 then to lag
	felt plane@g-load felt - dt lag / 1 min2 * + plane!g-felt ;

\ how much of the pilot's sight the felt g has taken, 0 to 1: none below the grey-out's g, all at the black-out's
: vision-loss ( plane -- share )
	| plane |
	plane :g-felt 1 @or grey-out-g - black-out-g grey-out-g - / 0 1 clamp ;

\ the stall's buffet: 0 where it begins, the type's buffet margin below the stall angle, rising to 1 at the stall and beyond, either way; none on the ground
: buffet-share ( plane -- share )
	| plane |
	plane@on-ground if 0 exit then
	plane@type to type
	plane@angle-of-attack abs type@stall-angle type@buffet-margin - - type@buffet-margin / 0 1 clamp ;

\ the flaps moving toward the selector's position over their travel time; blown back up when the indicated speed passes the blow-back speed
: move-flaps ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@flaps plane indicated-speed plane flap-blow-speed > and if
		false plane!flaps
		true plane!flaps-blown
	then
	plane@flaps if 1 else 0 then to target
	dt flap-travel-time / to step
	plane@flap-setting target - abs step <= if
		target plane!flap-setting
	else
		plane@flap-setting target plane@flap-setting > if step else step negate then + plane!flap-setting
	then ;

\ the undercarriage moving toward the selector's position, 1 down and locked, 0 up, over its travel time
: move-undercarriage ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@gear if 1 else 0 then to target
	dt undercarriage-travel-time / to step
	plane@gear-position target - abs step <= if
		target plane!gear-position
	else
		plane@gear-position target plane@gear-position > if step else step negate then + plane!gear-position
	then ;

\ the air's temperature at a height, °C: 15 at sea level, falling 6.5 a kilometre
: air-temperature ( height -- celsius ) 0.0065 * 15 swap - ;

\ the engine's step: the coolant heated by the power and cooled by the air through the radiator, less as the glycol leaks away; the oil's temperature following it, the oil's pressure with the revolutions, less as the oil warms and leaks; the wear of running hot or short of oil, the engine seizing when it is worn out
: run-engine ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@power to power
	plane@position second air-temperature to outside
	plane air-velocity v-length plane@revolutions propeller-wash * + to airflow
	plane@coolant-left plane@coolant-leak dt * - 0 max2 plane!coolant-left
	plane@oil-left plane@oil-leak dt * - 0 max2 plane!oil-left
	plane@coolant-left to coolant
	plane@coolant-temperature to temperature
	temperature 60 - 15 / 0 1 clamp to thermostat
	engine-heat power * airflow coolant * thermostat * temperature outside - * - coolant-capacity coolant 0.1 max2 * / dt * temperature + to temperature
	temperature plane!coolant-temperature
	plane@oil-temperature temperature 0.75 * plane@oil-temperature - dt * oil-lag / + to oil
	oil plane!oil-temperature
	plane@engine-dead if 0 else
		oil-full-pressure plane@revolutions sqrt * oil 70 - 0.3 * - 0 max2 plane@oil-left 3 * 1 min2 *
	then to pressure
	pressure plane!oil-pressure
	plane@engine-dead if exit then
	temperature coolant-limit > if
		plane@engine-wear temperature coolant-limit - coolant-boiling coolant-limit - / dt * 60 / + plane!engine-wear
	then
	temperature coolant-boiling > if plane@engine-wear dt 10 / + plane!engine-wear then
	pressure oil-low-pressure < plane@revolutions 0.1 > and if
		plane@engine-wear oil-low-pressure pressure - oil-low-pressure / dt * 30 / + plane!engine-wear
	then
	plane@engine-wear 1 >= if
		true plane!engine-dead
		temperature coolant-limit > if "THE GLYCOL HAS BOILED AND THE ENGINE HAS SEIZED" else "THE OIL PRESSURE HAS GONE AND THE ENGINE HAS SEIZED" then plane!engine-failure
	then ;

\ the aircraft's rotation for a step: the moments, less the gyroscopic coupling of its three rotations, turn it through its moments of inertia, the fuel's share of them as the tanks hold it; worked in the right-handed axes along the span, the vertical and the line of flight, where pitching up, rolling right and yawing right are a negative, a negative and a positive turn. The attitude then turns by the rates
: rotate-aircraft ( plane moments dt -- )
	| plane moments dt |
	plane@type to type
	plane@fuel plane@fuel-capacity / to fuel-share
	type@dry-inertia type@fuel-inertia fuel-share v* v+ spread to yaw-inertia to pitch-inertia to roll-inertia
	plane@rates spread to yaw-rate to pitch-rate to roll-rate
	moments spread to yaw-moment to pitch-moment to roll-moment
	pitch-rate negate to across-spin
	yaw-rate to vertical-spin
	roll-rate negate to along-spin
	pitch-moment negate roll-inertia yaw-inertia - vertical-spin * along-spin * - pitch-inertia / dt * across-spin + to across-spin
	yaw-moment pitch-inertia roll-inertia - along-spin * across-spin * - yaw-inertia / dt * vertical-spin + to vertical-spin
	roll-moment negate yaw-inertia pitch-inertia - across-spin * vertical-spin * - roll-inertia / dt * along-spin + to along-spin
	across-spin negate to pitch-rate
	vertical-spin to yaw-rate
	along-spin negate to roll-rate
	plane@attitude to attitude
	[ roll-rate pitch-rate yaw-rate ] plane!rates
	attitude :right pitch-rate dt * negate turn-attitude
	:forward roll-rate dt * negate turn-attitude
	:up yaw-rate dt * turn-attitude
	square-attitude to attitude
	plane attitude :attitude ! drop ;

\ the aircraft against the surface after a step: a crash if the propeller's lowest blade tip or, with the wheels not locked down, the fuselage's bottom goes below it; on the wheels a crash over the water or on a hillside outside a level place, below the carrier's deck, coming down faster than 4 m/s or banked so a wing tip meets the surface; else on the ground while a wheel touches. The land and water judged by the water's region nearest the aircraft
: touch-ground ( plane touching -- )
	| plane touching |
	plane@position to position
	position on-deck? to over-deck
	plane over-deck :on-deck ! drop
	position surface-height to ground
	over-deck position second ground 2 - < and if plane true :crashed ! "flew into the carrier's side" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	plane plane@type@hub body-arm position v+ [ 0 1.64 0 ] v- to blade-tip
	blade-tip second blade-tip surface-height < if plane true :crashed ! "the propeller struck the ground" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	plane@gear-position 1 < if
		plane [ 0 plane@type@belly plane centre-of-mass 2 @i ] body-arm position v+ to belly
		belly second belly surface-height < if
			plane true :crashed ! plane@gear-position 0 > if "landed before the wheels locked down" else "landed with the wheels up" then :crash-reason ! drop exit
		then
	then
	touching 0 = if plane false :on-ground ! drop exit then
	plane@on-ground not to arriving
	plane@velocity plane surface-velocity v- second to sinking
	plane@attitude@right second abs to bank
	position use-water-near
	over-deck not position first position 2 @i water-at? and if plane true :crashed ! "ditched in the sea" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	over-deck not ground 0 > and position first position 2 @i level-place-at null? and if plane true :crashed ! "flew into the hillside" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	arriving sinking -4 < and bank 0.37 > or if plane true :crashed ! "struck the ground" :crash-reason ! drop exit then
	plane true :on-ground ! drop ;

\ a step's fuel, burned in proportion to the power from the tanks the cock selects: the main tanks, or the reserve where the type has one; the engine starved while the selected tanks are dry
: burn-fuel ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@power full-power-flow * 3600 / dt * to burn
	plane@reserve-fuel to reserve
	plane@fuel reserve - to main
	plane@fuel-cock :reserve = if
		reserve burn - 0 max2 to reserve
		reserve plane!reserve-fuel
	else
		main burn - 0 max2 to main
	then
	main reserve + plane!fuel
	plane@fuel-cock :reserve = if reserve else main then 0 <= plane!fuel-starved ;

\ one step of flight, dt seconds; the propeller turned on by its revolutions through the reduction gear
: fly ( plane dt -- )
	| plane dt |
	plane@crashed if exit then
	plane dt :dt ! drop
	plane dt burn-fuel
	plane@engine-dead if 0 plane!throttle then
	plane dt move-air
	plane dt run-propeller
	plane@propeller-angle plane@revolutions rated-revolutions * propeller-gear * TAU * 60 / dt * + TAU mod plane!propeller-angle
	plane dt move-controls
	plane aerodynamics to moments to force
	plane@gear-position 1 >= if plane wheel-forces else [ 0 0 0 ] [ 0 0 0 ] 0 then to touching to wheel-moments to wheel-force
	force wheel-force v+ to force
	moments wheel-moments v+ to moments
	force plane aircraft-mass 1 swap / v* to acceleration
	plane@velocity acceleration dt v* v+ to velocity
	plane velocity :velocity ! drop
	plane plane@position velocity dt v* v+ :position ! drop
	acceleration [ 0 gravity 0 ] v+ plane@attitude@up v-dot gravity / plane!g-load
	plane@attitude@forward heading-of to old-heading
	plane dt move-flaps
	plane dt move-undercarriage
	plane dt feel-g
	plane dt run-engine
	plane moments dt rotate-aircraft
	plane@attitude@forward heading-of old-heading - 540 + 360 mod 180 - dt / plane!turn-rate
	plane touching touch-ground ;
